hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1388.1",""
"1388.2",""
"1389.1","[1389] Malik told me that he had heard that a man came to Abdullah bin Omar and said: O Abu Abdul Rahman, I have asked A man who is a predecessor, and I made a better condition for him than what I made. Abdullah bin Omar said: That is usury. He said: How do you command me, O Abu Abdul Rahman? Abdullah said: Advances are based on three aspects: a predecessor is a predecessor seeking the face of God, so you have the face of God, and a predecessor is a predecessor you seek. If you want the honor of your companion, you will have the honor of your companion, and an advance you will give in order to take what is evil for what is good. That is usury. He said: How do you command me, O Abu Abdul Rahman? He said: I think that you should tear the paper in pieces, and if he gives you the same as what you borrowed, you accept it, and if he gives you less than what you borrowed. So you take him and you will be rewarded, and if he gives you better than what you asked him for and is good to himself, then that is his gratitude to you, and to you The reward for what you have seen.\\nMota in front of Malik Hadith: 1389 Arabic Hadith:"
"1389.2","[1389] Malik told me that he had heard that a man came to Abdullah bin Omar and said: O Abu Abdul Rahman, I have asked A man who is a predecessor, and I made a better condition for him than what I made. Abdullah bin Omar said: That is usury. He said: How do you command me, O Abu Abdul Rahman? Abdullah said: Advances are based on three aspects: a predecessor is a predecessor seeking the face of God, so you have the face of God, and a predecessor is a predecessor you seek. If you want the honor of your companion, you will have the honor of your companion, and an advance you will give in order to take what is evil for what is good. That is usury. He said: How do you command me, O Abu Abdul Rahman? He said: I think that you should tear the paper in pieces, and if he gives you the same as what you borrowed, you accept it, and if he gives you less than what you borrowed. So you take him and you will be rewarded, and if he gives you better than what you asked him for and is good to himself, then that is his gratitude to you, and to you The reward for what you have seen.\\nMota in front of Malik Hadith: 1389 Arabic Hadith:"
